    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-a-non-claude-node-in-a-c-31": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-a-non-claude-node-in-a-c-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "A non-Claude node in a container run "
        },
        {
          "insert": "fails fast before any node executes",
          "attributes": {
            "bold": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", naming the provider and the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "containerExec",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " capability: never a silent host escape."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-security-posture-32": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-security-posture-32",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Security posture"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-the-container-is-the-iso-33": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-the-container-is-the-iso-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The container is the isolation boundary: read-only lower bind + overlay upper on a VM-local volume + Archon-managed env only (the host "
        },
        {
          "insert": "process.env",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " never crosses) + approval-gated write-back. The apply is the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "one",
          "attributes": {
            "bold": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " moment the live root is written."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-overlay-mount-mode-is-ch-34": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-overlay-mount-mode-is-ch-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Overlay mount mode is chosen least-privilege-first: "
        },
        {
          "insert": "fuse-overlayfs",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " (only "
        },
        {
          "insert": "--device /dev/fuse",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", no "
        },
        {
          "insert": "CAP_SYS_ADMIN",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ") is attempted first and works on rootless / userns-remap daemons. Native fallback requires the runner to report a FUSE mount failure with exit status 78. Docker startup failures, including a missing "
        },
        {
          "insert": "/dev/fuse",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", readiness timeouts, unconfirmed exit status, and failed log checks stop preparation without fallback. A transient inspection failure can recover: a later inspection confirming exit 78 permits fallback. On a standard rootful daemon, a confirmed FUSE mount failure permits "
        },
        {
          "insert": "native",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " (kernel overlay + "
        },
        {
          "insert": "CAP_SYS_ADMIN",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "), which lets in-container root remount the read-only lower, so "
        },
        {
          "insert": "native",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is isolation-hardening, "
        },
        {
          "insert": "not",
          "attributes": {
            "bold": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " a sandbox against a hostile agent. The full threat model, the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "native",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " caveat, and the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "docker exec -e",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " secrets-in-"
        },
        {
          "insert": "ps",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " limitation are documented in "
        },
        {
          "insert": "packages/isolation/docker/SECURITY.md",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ": read it before running untrusted work."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-two-archon-owned-host-di-35": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-two-archon-owned-host-di-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Two Archon-owned host directories are bound into the container at their host paths: the run's frozen workflow source, read-only, and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "$ARTIFACTS_DIR",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", read-write. The artifacts bind is what lets an in-container node leave screenshots, reports, or the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "evidence_policy",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " marker where the engine and the operator read them. It is the only host directory the container can write; its contents are run output and the host never executes them. Archon's own host writes into it refuse a symlinked parent directory and replace a symlinked file instead of writing through it, so a link planted by a node cannot redirect an engine write elsewhere on the host. On a rootful Linux daemon the container's root user owns what it writes there, so Archon attempts to restore host ownership when the container is suspended or destroyed. This is best-effort: failures are logged as "
        },
        {
          "insert": "isolation.container_artifacts_chown_failed",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and do not stop suspension or destruction."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-before-a-named-script-or-36": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-before-a-named-script-or-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Before a named script or other capture-backed read is dispatched, Archon hashes the host capture and compares it with the run's pinned source identity. This refuses a mismatch present at that checkpoint; it does not close the check-to-exec race, contain a same-UID host process, or cancel parallel nodes already running."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-macos-linux-notes-37": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-macos-linux-notes-37",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "macOS / Linux notes"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-70-container-isolation-the-overlay-upper-work-d-38": {
      "id": "b-70-container-isolation-the-overlay-upper-work-d-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The overlay upper/work dirs live on a VM-local named volume, never a host bind mount (a host-bind upperdir hits "
        },
        {
          "insert": "EACCES",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " on macOS). The merged overlay is mounted at the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "same absolute path",
          "attributes": {
            "bold": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " as the host cwd, so "
        },
        {
          "insert": "working_path",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and every path substitution are unchanged inside the container. On a standard Docker Desktop / rootful Engine daemon, "
        },
        {
          "insert": "native",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " (CAP"
        },
        {
          "insert": "SYS",
          "attributes": {
            "italic": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "ADMIN) is available only after the runner starts and reports the dedicated FUSE mount failure status; a missing "
        },
        {
          "insert": "/dev/fuse",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " stops the run before that point."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    }
  }
